// Sources and calculations are listed with each entry.
//
// Each entry is one variant of an item (e.g. item 'Beef': steak, burger). The first
// entry of an item is its default, used when comparing against it.
// Each entry gives either:
//   g:   [low, central, high] grams CO2e per unit, and/or
//   kwh: [low, central, high] kWh per unit; grams = g + kWh × grid intensity.
// A null centre means the sources give a range only, unless calc supplies the centre.
// [low, high] gives the low and high sourced estimates. Where an entry has both g and kwh, each
// position is one scenario (e.g. small car: little fuel, more electricity), so kwh alone need not be in order.
// low == high means only one sourced figure exists, so no range is shown.
// Entries with km: give g or kwh per passenger-km. km is the default one-way distance.
// The note gives the area covered by the average or identifies the example. With amt and unit,
// the figures are for that amount (e.g. 200 g). Other entries count units. avg describes the default
// amount, e.g. 'average' or 'example trip'. remote uses the world grid for all kwh.
// remoteKwh is a separate world-grid component when kwh uses the local grid.
// deviceScenarios gives paired manufacture, local electricity and remote electricity values
// when the devices that set the range can change with the grid.
// unc grades the evidence for the average. Compare only independent world or international
// estimates of the same quantity, scope and era. Variation across farms, devices and scenarios
// belongs in Range. 10: physics or direct measurement of the stated quantity; 25: three or more
// comparable estimates agree, or a verified study of the named model; 50: comparable averages
// within 1.5×; 100: up to 2× apart; 200: more than 2× apart.
// 'single': one usable international estimate or model, with its own confidence label.
// 'limited': a proxy, incomplete evidence or a narrow sample for the typical value.
// 'range': no comparable average. Range-only, single-source, limited and numeric grades 100+ are opt-in comparisons.
// rangeLabel explains the basis of the bounds. estimate labels a worked example in Average view.
// region: a country's own figure, listed only when that country's grid is chosen.
// calc: [factor, amount, what] terms; the central figure is the sum of amount × FACTORS[factor].g
// (g[1] is then null). made: [g, what], g one figure or [low, central, high]: making the vehicle or device,
// spread over its life, per unit. Break-even calculations use the full upfront footprint. checks: [title, url, grams] whole-item estimates by others, to compare against.

